@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# CybX Mobile 0.4.0
+
+CybX Mobile is a rootless Android ARM64 Linux workstation prototype. It runs a Kali NetHunter Minimal ARM64 userspace through an Android ARM64 PRoot executable and uses the Android host kernel. It is not a bootable replacement OS and does not grant Android root.
+
+## Changes in 0.4.0
+
+1. Rootfs selection no longer asks the user to paste a SHA-256. The app hashes the selected archive automatically and compares it with the pinned digest for the official Kali NetHunter Minimal ARM64 rootfs used by CybX Runtime Bundle v1.0.
+2. Added CybX launcher branding and adaptive/round launcher icons.
+3. Rootfs readiness now checks `/etc/os-release`, `env`, and `bash`, instead of only checking the release file. Extraction is staged and activated with rollback to the prior rootfs if activation fails.
+4. Replaced one-command-per-process output with Termux's Apache-2.0 terminal-view and terminal-emulator libraries. The terminal uses a PTY, persistent interactive Bash process, VT/ANSI emulation, resize support, clipboard hooks, and extra-key buttons. Shell state (for example `cd`) persists while the app process/session remains alive. Android may still reclaim background processes; a dedicated foreground service/session manager is a future hardening step.
+5. Added release-signing configuration driven by GitHub Actions secrets. Debug builds are not a stable update channel; see `SIGNING_AND_PLAY_PROTECT.md`.
+6. Kept permissions minimal: INTERNET and ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE only. No SMS, accessibility-service, notification-listener, or device-admin permissions are requested.
+
+## Runtime setup
+
+1. Download and extract `CybX-ARM64-Runtime-Bundle-v1.0.zip`.
+2. In CybX Mobile, open Linux Setup and select `kali-nethunter-rootfs-minimal-arm64.tar.xz`.
+3. CybX calculates SHA-256 automatically. Only the pinned official archive is accepted. The app then validates the rootfs layout and activates it.
+4. Open Terminal. Try `uname -m`, `cat /etc/os-release`, `pwd`, `cd /tmp`, and `pwd`.
+
+The rootfs is app-private. Uninstalling CybX removes it, so keep the original `.tar.xz` archive outside the app.
+
+## Android W^X compatibility
+
+The app currently uses target SDK 28 because Android's app-data executable restrictions affect PRoot guest binaries on modern Android. This follows the execution model currently used by Termux. The app is compiled against API 35. This target level is a technical compatibility choice for the rootless PRoot prototype, not a claim of Google Play submission compliance. A Play Store build needs a separately engineered modern target-SDK execution path and full device testing.
+
+## Build
+
+The GitHub Actions workflow builds `app-source-v0.4.0.zip`. Without a configured release keystore it creates a debug APK for testing. For repeatable in-place updates, configure the four GitHub Actions repository secrets documented in `SIGNING_AND_PLAY_PROTECT.md` and use the signed release APK. The first v0.4.0 install will still require replacing older builds if their ephemeral debug signing certificates differ.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- PRoot is user-space emulation and shares Android's kernel.
+- Kernel features requiring root or device-specific kernel support (monitor mode, raw packet injection, gadget/HID, privileged networking) are not guaranteed.
+- The terminal has a real PTY and VT emulator, but sessions are still owned by the app process; background service persistence and multi-session management are future items.
+- Rootfs digest is pinned to the CybX Runtime Bundle v1.0 archive. A future official rootfs update must update the pinned digest deliberately.
+- Checksum matching verifies exact bytes. It does not replace publisher provenance review or Android app signing.
+
+## Third-party components
+
+- PRoot portable Android ARM64 binary: packaged from the existing CybX runtime bundle with upstream provenance noted in that bundle.
+- Termux `terminal-view` and `terminal-emulator` 0.118.0: Apache License 2.0. Upstream source: https://github.com/termux/termux-app
